INT. COUNTRY HOUSE DINING ROOM - 1840S - EVENING

A long mahogany table laid for three. Silver, crystal, candles. A fire crackles. A portrait of a stern ancestor presides over the sideboard.

AGNES, forties, in dove-gray silk, sits at the head. MR. FOTHERGILL, fifties, a visiting solicitor, to her right, with a napkin tucked in his collar. EDMUND, twenty, her son, sits opposite him, turning a wine glass by its stem.

She lifts the soup tureen herself. Ladles three portions. Her hand is quite steady.

FOTHERGILL
This is excellent.

AGNES
Cook will be pleased.

He samples it. She watches him. Edmund watches the glass.

FOTHERGILL
Mrs. Whitcombe, I regret I have news.

AGNES
Mr. Fothergill, you always have news. Eat first.

He eats. A pause. The fire pops.

EDMUND
Mother, I think perhaps we should hear it.

AGNES
After soup.

EDMUND
But if it concerns--

AGNES
After soup, Edmund.

He subsides. Sets down his glass. The candle flames shiver as a servant's footsteps pass in the hall and fade.

FOTHERGILL
The estate. The creditors have called in the note.

Agnes sets down her spoon. Aligns it with the edge of her bowl.

AGNES
How much?

FOTHERGILL
Four thousand pounds.

EDMUND
We don't have four thousand.

FOTHERGILL
I know.

EDMUND
Then we'll sell the east pasture.

AGNES
The east pasture was your father's.

EDMUND
Father is dead.

She looks at him. He drops his eyes. Fothergill coughs into his napkin.

FOTHERGILL
There is another way.

AGNES
There always is.

FOTHERGILL
A marriage. A gentleman in Bath, a widower of good family, has inquired--

AGNES
(to Edmund)
Pass the bread.

EDMUND
Mother.

AGNES
The bread, Edmund.

He passes it. She tears off a piece, takes her time buttering it. Fothergill waits, napkin twisted in his fist.

FOTHERGILL
It would settle the debt.

AGNES
And whom does the gentleman wish to marry?

Fothergill looks, slowly, at Edmund. Edmund stares back, color draining from his face. Agnes, not looking at either of them, takes a bite of bread.

AGNES (CONT'D)
(mildly)
I see. More soup, Mr. Fothergill?

He holds out his bowl. She fills it. He takes it with a trembling hand and does not meet her eyes. Edmund rises.

EDMUND
Excuse me.

AGNES
Sit down.

He doesn't. He walks to the fire and stands with his back to the table, his shoulders very straight.

FOTHERGILL
(clearing his throat)
The offer remains open until Friday.

AGNES
Then we shall eat our dinner, and sleep, and tomorrow I shall write to Bath.

FOTHERGILL
Madam?

AGNES
Whatever I write, Mr. Fothergill, is my own business.

She resumes her soup. At the fire, Edmund lifts a hand and wipes his eyes, quickly, with the back of his wrist.
